Each spec file that contains tests must start with import of the test function from PW:
import {test} from '@playwright/test'

Each test function requires certain arguments:
test('My Frist Test', () => {

})

Single test can be orgainzed in test suits:
test.describe('Test suite A', () => {
    test('My Frist Test', () => {
        Test Body
})

    test('My Second Test', () => {
        Test Body
})

    test('My Third Test', () => {
        Test Body
})
})

page is a fuxture (there are 4 in total) and it represents a blank page in a browser that will be used for test.
We can call it and use some of many methods on it.
Throught the page fixture we call different PW methods that let us execute tests.

test('My Frist Test', async ({page}) => {
        await page.goto('https://playground.bondaracademy.com/pages/iot-dashboard');
        await page.getByText('Forms').click();
        await page.getByText('Page Layouts').click();
})

Type of the goto method is promise. In typescript this type can have 2 outcomes:
resolved - excecuted successfully
rejected - there was attempt to resolve it within certain amount of time (timeout), but it was unseccessful.
Whenever a method/function is of promise type, it must be preceeded by await keyword.

async is the type of the callback function.

page.locator() is an example of a function that is not a promise and await will have no effect on it (a hint will say so if we add it).

getByText() and click() are chained together and will be excecuted one after another. The first one is of type Locator and does not
require an await, but click() and so it must be added.